# tests/test-scripts.sh — helper contract

Cited when writing or reviewing cases in `tests/test-scripts.sh`. Not hot context.

Written after `ds-hygiene-hook` (v2026.8.9) re-derived this three times — once while
planning, twice under code review — at roughly a review round each.

## What each helper actually asserts

| Helper | Exit code | stdout | stderr |
|---|---|---|---|
| `check` | must be 0 | discarded | discarded |
| `check_fails` | must be non-zero | discarded | discarded |
| `check_exit <desc> <code> <substr>` | must equal `<code>` | discarded | grepped for `<substr>` |

Three consequences that have each cost a round:

1. **A script under test must write diagnostics to stderr** if a case wants to assert the
   message. `check_exit` greps stderr only. `gate.sh` captures both streams, so writing to
   stderr costs nothing in implementer feedback.
2. **There is no absence assertion.** `check_exit` proves a substring is present, never that
   one is missing. Asserting "X is not flagged" needs an inline `bash -c`:

       check "desc" bash -c 'out=$(bash hook 2>&1); ! grep -q needle <<<"$out"'

3. **There is no emptiness assertion, and `$(...)` discards exit status.** This shape is a
   trap — it passes for a command that exits 1 silently:

       check "desc" bash -c '[ -z "$(cmd 2>&1 >/dev/null)" ]'          # status ignored

   Capture both when the criterion names both:

       check "desc" bash -c 'err=$(cmd 2>&1 >/dev/null); s=$?; [[ $s -eq 0 && -z "$err" ]]'

## A green suite is not a real suite

Every vacuous test found in this repo has passed the gate. The gate cannot distinguish a
test that proves something from one that cannot fail.

Before trusting a case that asserts a guard exists, delete the guard and re-run. If the
suite stays green, the case is decoration. Three cases in `ds-hygiene-hook` failed this and
were caught only by reviewers doing it by hand.

Common vacuity: a fixture that violates two checks at once, asserted with a single non-zero
exit. The assertion cannot tell which check fired, so it survives the deletion of either.
Give each guard a fixture that violates **only** the thing that guard controls.

A second vacuity shape, from `gate-tool-preflight` (v2026.8.10): a fixture too impoverished to
exhibit the behaviour at all. The case asserted the gate's preflight aborts *before* running
checks, by grepping for absence of the `▶` run marker — but the fixture had no runnable tool,
so no implementation could ever print one. Deleting the abort left the suite green. When
asserting that something does **not** appear, first confirm the fixture can produce it.

## The suite runs *inside* the gate, so environment hermeticity is bidirectional

`scripts/gate.d/test-scripts.sh` runs this suite as a gate hook, so `bash scripts/gate.sh`
executes the suite, which executes fixture gates. Any variable `gate.sh` reads therefore
flows in both directions, and both cost a review round in `gate-tool-preflight`:

- **Outbound:** a case that `export`s such a variable leaks it into the nested gate run and
  into every later case. Set it per-command instead — `VAR=x bash -c '…'`.
- **Inbound:** a value already in the environment reaches the hook and then every fixture.
  `GATE_REQUIRED_TOOLS=shellcheck bash scripts/gate.sh` failed 8 of 15 new cases this way —
  nothing was broken; the inherited requirement aborted each fixture gate before it could
  run, because fixtures deliberately use a restricted `PATH`.

Neutralise the inbound direction once, structurally, next to the suite's `cd "$ROOT"` rather
than immediately above the affected block — a guard that works only because of where it sits
is silently lost by the next case added above it:

    unset GATE_REQUIRED_TOOLS   # the suite runs nested inside gate.sh; see above

Check both directions when adding a variable `gate.sh` reads: `grep -n export tests/test-scripts.sh`
for outbound, and one full-suite run with the variable set for inbound.

## Proving cross-platform behaviour on one platform

The gate runs on whichever platform invoked it, so a portability bug can sit behind a fully
green suite. `ds-hygiene-hook` shipped a `stat` probe to review that worked on macOS and
silently disabled its check on every Linux box.

Where behaviour branches on a tool's platform variant, stub the other variant on `PATH` and
run against it, rather than reasoning about the flags:

    # GNU-shaped stat: -c succeeds, -f fails
    printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$1" in -c) echo 21;; *) exit 1;; esac\n' >"$stub/stat"
    chmod +x "$stub/stat"
    PATH="$stub:$PATH" bash scripts/gate.d/the-hook.sh

Note the failure that made this necessary: GNU `stat -f %z` does **not** fail. `-f` is
`--file-system` there, an unrecognised directive prints `?` and exits 0. Probes of the form
"try the BSD flag, fall back if it fails" are therefore inverted; order the GNU form first
and fall back to the BSD one, which does exit non-zero on an unknown flag.
# Silent no-ops: cwd- and checkout-dependence

Cited when writing or reviewing a script that resolves paths, or when mutation-testing a guard.
Not hot context.

Written after `work-artifact-capture` (v2026.8.11) hit the same failure family **four times in one
unit** — three in the code under review, one in the orchestrator's own dispatch. Six review rounds;
the suite was green through all of them.

## The family

A command runs, does nothing or does less than intended, and **exits 0**. Nothing fails, so nothing
alerts. This is worse than a crash in exactly the places the loop trusts an exit code.

The four instances, all from one unit:

| Instance | Symptom |
|---|---|
| `sync-artifacts` run from inside the worktree | `ROOT` resolved to the worktree, copied nothing, exit 0 |
| `codex-review.sh` diff pathspec `--  .` | From a subdirectory, reviewer's diff silently truncated |
| Same pathspec missing `,top` on the exclude | Exclusion stopped matching; eight artifact files leaked back in |
| `scripts/agent-exec.sh` with a stale shell cwd | Dispatch never ran; outer command still reported exit 0 |

The third one is the sharpest: a reviewer receives a diff missing most of the change, finds nothing
objectionable, exits 0, and the loop **mechanically records an APPROVE over a diff that was never
shown**. An empty or short diff is not a git error, so `die`-style handling never fires.

## The idioms that fix them

- **Anchor pathspecs to the repo root, not the cwd.** `git diff … -- ':/' ':(exclude,top)work/<slug>'`.
  `:/` includes from the root; **`,top` is required** on the exclude or it gets the cwd prefix applied
  and quietly stops matching. Verify by comparing output from the root and from a subdirectory —
  identical, or the anchor is wrong.
- **Ask "am I in the primary checkout", not "where is `main`".**
  `[[ "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)" == "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" ]]` is true only in the
  primary checkout, and is immune to whichever branch happens to be checked out. Resolving by branch
  name instead gives both false refusals (a small fix left on a branch blocks a release) and false
  acceptances (`main` checked out in a linked worktree).
- **`cd "$ROOT"` early**, as `worktree.sh` does, or accept that every relative path in the script is
  caller-dependent. Do not do half of this.
- **Dispatch with absolute paths.** Shell cwd persists across an orchestrator's commands; a relative
  `scripts/…` resolves against wherever the last probe left it.
- **Filenames are not text.** `git diff --cached --name-only` C-quotes non-ASCII paths, and feeding
  those back as pathspecs fails (`pathspec '"work/…/r\303\251sum\303\251.md"' did not match`) —
  after `git add` has already run, so the tree is left dirty. Use a pathspec on both the guard and
  the commit instead of round-tripping through filenames.

## Assert your mutation actually landed

Mutation testing is the only thing that has caught vacuous tests in this repo — but a mutation that
*fails to apply* produces a false green indistinguishable from a passing guard. This happened
**twice** in one unit, nearly letting both a vacuous test and an unpinned guard ship.

Before trusting red-or-green, prove the edit is in the file:

    # after editing, assert the old text is gone and the new text is present
    grep -c 'exclude,top' scripts/codex-review.sh   # expect 0 after mutating it out
    git diff --stat                                  # expect a non-empty diff

A reviewer's `sed`/`python3` replace that silently matched nothing looks exactly like a well-pinned
guard. The check costs one line.

## Two vacuity shapes seen here

Both are in `knowledge/test-helper-contract.md` too; repeated because this unit produced one of each.

1. **A fixture too impoverished to exhibit the behaviour.** A case asserted the gate aborts *before*
   running checks, by grepping for the absence of a run marker — in a fixture with no runnable tool,
   where no implementation could ever print one.
2. **An assertion the environment satisfies regardless.** A `.DS_Store` placed in the *worktree* was
   asserted absent from the tree — but gitignore excludes it under every implementation, so the
   staging strategy it was meant to pin had no test behind it at all. The fix was a *non-dot*
   gitignored stray in the *primary* directory.

## An empty "nothing differs" check

Seen in `docs-stage` (v2026.10.0). To decide whether a squash-merged `wt/<slug>` branch had
landed, the orchestrator ran `files=$(git diff --name-only <base> <branch>)` and then
`git diff --stat origin/main <branch> -- $files`. The Bash tool's shell is **zsh**, which does not
word-split an unquoted `$files`. The whole newline-joined list became one pathspec that matched
nothing, the diff printed nothing, and "no output" was read as "fully landed". A worktree holding
unlanded review fixes was then removed.

- When empty output means "safe to delete", make the check able to print something: run
  `git diff --stat origin/main <branch>` over the whole tree first, and only then narrow it.
- Do not rely on unquoted word-splitting in tool-run shell snippets. Use `${=files}` in zsh, an
  array, or pipe the list through `xargs`.
---
name: 4-release
description: Run the release script for a work unit, then push, PR, and tag autonomously (full TRIP).
---

# /4-release — release

Input: a work unit slug.

## Steps

1. **Run the release script:** from the repo root, run `scripts/worktree.sh sync-artifacts <slug>`, then `bash scripts/release.sh <slug> --confirm-delta "none"` (full TRIP: no in-session confirmation; `Confirm-delta` is logged as `none`).
2. **React to the exit code:**
   - Exit 0 → report the bump commit on the release branch, that no tag exists yet, and that `main` was untouched.
   - Non-zero → report the script output and stop.
3. **Check README drift:** confirm README's loop, skills and Layout sections still match the shipped skills and scripts; fix drift via the small-fix path, never mentioning dogfood-only scripts (README ships in exported templates).
4. **Push the release branch and open the PR** against `main` autonomously; never push `main` directly. `main` stays protected, so a PR + merge is still required.
5. **After the PR merges,** run `bash scripts/release.sh tag-after-merge <slug>`. If it exits non-zero, report the output and stop. If it succeeds, run `git push origin v<version>`.
6. **Invoke `/5-retro`** for the released unit.

## Rules

- After the release PR merges, check each primary-checkout untracked `work/<slug>/` copy against `origin/main`, then delete matching copies before `git pull --ff-only`.

- The release script is the source of truth for the release result.
- Full TRIP: the Orchestrator runs the release, push, PR, and tag without an in-session Owner confirmation. (The harness may still independently gate the PR merge into protected `main`; that is a platform guard, not a framework confirmation.)
# Architecture

_A snapshot of what the codebase IS — regenerated by `/init` and `/compact`, never hand-groomed._

## Stack

Bash (`set -uo pipefail`, macOS/Linux) and Markdown. No compiled language, no package manager. The "runtime" is Claude Code itself: skills orchestrate, agents review, shell scripts do the deterministic work. The implementer runtime is a one-line `config.yaml` swap (`codex` here; `claude` under the `work` profile). The gate declares `shellcheck` as a required tool.

## Layout

- `CLAUDE.md` / `ARCHI.md` / `PLAN.md` — the hot context tier (loaded every session, ~300-line budget).
- `config.yaml` — the single knob: profile, per-role models (incl. `fan_selector`), implementer runtime, `implementer.fan` (best-of-N sample count; default 1 = single-agent), `reviewer.command` (the codex second reviewer, read from `main:config.yaml` at review time — branch config cannot choose the reviewer binary), gate command and required tools, optional `worktrees.dir` (defaults to `../<checkout-basename>-worktrees`).
- `skills/` — the loop stages (`1-plan`…`5-retro`) plus `init`, `compact`, and `docs`. Each is a `SKILL.md`; `1-plan`, `2-implement`, and `docs` carry `prompts/*.tpl` (plan, handoff, followup; docs handoff). `5-retro` closes each unit: four questions, each lesson routed to exactly one of `mechanical`/`contextual`/`process`/`not worth keeping`, applied on a `wt/retro-<slug>` worktree (reused if it already exists; removed after the PR merges) that may touch only `knowledge/`, `PLAN.md`, and the retro file. `2-implement` also documents Fan Mode (N>1): dispatch N samples, gate-filter, adopt a winner. `skills/docs` is the `/docs` documentation-writing stage: it dispatches codex/GPT through the two-arg `agent-exec.sh` to author/rewrite prose, ships by the small-fix path, and runs no gate/no self-review (its handoff fills the footprint from the plan and switches off the AGENTS.md gate rule; the writer summary lands in `work/<slug>/notes.md`). Exposed to Claude Code via the `.claude/skills → ../skills` symlink.
- `.claude/agents/` — fresh-subagent definitions (`plan-reviewer.md`, `code-reviewer.md`, `fan-selector.md`): read-only tools, separate model, cold context.
- `scripts/` — the deterministic layer: `gate.sh` (stack-detecting check runner), `demo.sh` (runs the first sh/bash fence in a plan's `## Demonstration`, respecting headings only outside fences; plain bash from the requested git worktree, streaming output; exit 0 pass or `None:`, 1 block failure or porcelain-status delta, 2 plan/tooling error), `worktree.sh` (isolated-checkout lifecycle: `add` best-effort-fetches and branches new `wt/<slug>` from `origin/main` — HEAD only when no `origin/main` ref exists — then idempotently commits `work/<slug>/plan.md` onto the branch so the plan flows to release; `sync-artifacts` copies top-level regular non-dot primary `work/<slug>/` files, explicitly not symlinks, except `plan.md` into the worktree, then stages the whole `work/<slug>` directory minus `plan.md` so pre-existing worktree-side unit files are also captured, and commits only when staged unit changes exist; the resume path skips the fetch/base change), `agent-exec.sh` (dispatches the implementer into a worktree; normalizes the worktree path to absolute and fails `no new commit` only when the tree was clean before dispatch and a zero-exit dispatch leaves HEAD unchanged *and* the tree still clean — the produced-literally-nothing case), `codex-review.sh` (second-vendor code reviewer: pure reader run from the primary checkout, reads the plan body from the branch via `git show wt/<slug>:…` but `reviewer.command` from `main:config.yaml`, best-effort-fetches then diffs the branch against `origin/main` (falling back to local `main` only when no `refs/remotes/origin/main` ref exists) so a stale local `main` can't pollute the review, pipes plan+diff excluding `work/<slug>` artifacts to the read-only reviewer, captures stdout to a temp file and moves it to `work/<slug>/codex-review.md` only on reviewer exit 0; verdict contract exit 0 = APPROVE / 1 = REQUEST CHANGES / 2 = tooling error, never a verdict), `fan-exec.sh` (best-of-N: `dispatch` spawns N sample worktrees, seeds `work/<slug>/` uncommitted, auto-commits each sample before the gate, prints a manifest of gate-passers; `adopt` retargets `wt/<slug>` to a chosen sample and force-cleans all `<slug>-fan-*` worktrees/branches), `release.sh` (release preconditions incl. the previous unit's non-empty `retro.md` + version/changelog/commit + `work/.last-released` marker on the release branch — the marker must be a regular file byte-identical to `origin/main`'s copy (`cmp`, symlinks refused; on bootstrap with no origin marker, a local marker must be non-empty single-line); never tags, never touches `main`, never pushes; `tag-after-merge` tags post-merge; rollback resets and cleans the marker), `state.sh` (read-only per-work-unit execution-state deriver: given a slug, prints flat `slug`/`stage`/`review`/`next_action` lines derived purely from observable facts — artifact presence under `work/<slug>/`, the whole-line verdict sentinels read from the branch plan via `git show wt/<slug>:…` when the branch exists else the primary plan, the `wt/<slug>` ref, and the `work/.last-released` marker; `review` is `approve` only when both `Code-review verdict: APPROVE` and `Codex-review verdict: APPROVE` are present; seven first-match-wins stage rules `plan→implement→review→release→done`; gate result is deliberately not derived, so `next_action` names `run scripts/gate.sh` live; writes nothing), `archi-fresh.sh` (ref-aware ARCHI freshness check extracted from `release.sh`: `archi-fresh.sh [<ref>]` compares the last-commit epoch of `ARCHI.md` vs `scripts/ skills/ profiles/ config.yaml CLAUDE.md` at `<ref>`, default `HEAD`; strict `<` = stale so equal epochs pass; exit 0 fresh / 1 stale naming the newer paths / 2 missing history; `--help` prints usage. `release.sh check_archi_fresh` delegates to it and `/3-review` runs it against `wt/<slug>` to catch staleness before release). `export-template.sh <dest>` resolves `<dest>` against the caller's cwd, `cd`s to the repo root, exports HEAD via `git archive` and overlays committed skeletons via `git ls-tree`/`git show`; exit 0 success / 1 refusal / 2 tooling error. `gate.d/*.sh` are auto-run hooks (`test-scripts.sh` wires the smoke and export suites in); `gate.d/examples/` contains opt-in hygiene hooks for notebook outputs (`nb-clean.sh`) and data-science artifacts/local paths (`ds-hygiene.sh`).
- `VERSION` / `CHANGELOG.md` — CalVer (`YYYY.M.MICRO`) + Keep-a-Changelog; written only by `release.sh`.
- `profiles/` — `software` (base), `machine-learning` (eval-defines-done + notebook strategy + opt-in repo hygiene hook declaration for data/model artifacts and hardcoded local paths), `database`, `work` (Atlassian/Bitbucket: Claude implementer, Bitbucket CI, Jira reference-only, pointer to ML notebook/repo hygiene because profiles do not merge); selected by `config.yaml`'s `profile:`.
- `tests/test-scripts.sh` — smoke tests for the shell layer: release refusals + a rollback test (pre-commit-hook forced failure; the marker `git clean` insurance path itself is untestable at pre-commit granularity) + the PR-merge flow (bump-on-branch, `origin/main` collision guard, mid-gate re-check, `tag-after-merge` happy + wrong-commit refusal, refusal without either verdict sentinel, the retro refusal matrix + marker integrity cases (deleted/mutated/trailing-newline/symlinked/re-run markers, malformed bootstrap markers) + rollback cases), hermetic fan-exec cases (canned implementer over a real worktree + bare-remote topology), hermetic agent-exec cases (commit / uncommitted / produced-nothing / relative-path / cd-elsewhere / dirty-redispatch dispatches), `worktree.sh add` base/seed cases, `worktree.sh sync-artifacts` artifact-capture/refusal cases, `nb-clean.sh` and `ds-hygiene.sh` example-hook cases, hermetic demo cases (first-block/section scoping, headings inside fences, bash fences, cwd/default dir, plain bash, failure code, dirty-tree refusal, None, missing/empty plan sections, non-git directory, help/arguments), and hermetic codex-review cases (canned reviewer: exit-code matrix with stderr substrings, main-sourced config + ignored branch override, parser termination + commented-command anchor, artifact-only write, worktree stays clean, failed reviewer preserves the prior artifact, missing-plan and wrong-checkout refusals).
- `template/` — ARCHI/PLAN skeletons, header-only CHANGELOG, and bootstrap VERSION overlay; `.gitattributes` excludes instance paths and export tooling from archives. `tests/test-export.sh` builds committed fixtures from working-tree files, tests refusals and HEAD isolation, and runs exported gate/freshness checks.
- CI: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (GitHub) and `bitbucket-pipelines.yml` (Bitbucket) both run the smoke suite + gate; the `work` profile keeps the Bitbucket one.
- `knowledge/` — cold-tier docs, loaded only on citation (empty but for a README); `work/<slug>/` — one directory per work unit (`plan.md` etc.).
- `AGENTS.md` — the contract the implementer subagent reads; carries a build-discipline ladder (lazy/YAGNI: reuse → stdlib → native → installed dep → one line → minimum code, run after understanding the problem) so the implementer builds the leanest working diff within scope, with explicit validation/security/error-handling boundaries never simplified away.

## Entry points

- **The loop:** `/1-plan` → `/2-implement` → `/3-review` → `/4-release` → `/5-retro`. Invoked as skills from the orchestrator session; full TRIP — release, push, PR, and tag run without an in-session Owner confirm; `/4-release` checks README drift before pushing (README ships in exported templates, so it never names dogfood-only scripts). `/3-review` is dual-vendor: fresh Claude `code-reviewer` subagent + `scripts/codex-review.sh <slug>`, both APPROVE required, both sentinels recorded in one in-place commit on the worktree plan. Both review paths fetch and base the diff on `origin/main` (local-`main` fallback only when no `origin/main` ref exists), so a lagging local `main` cannot pollute the review diff. Both reviewers gate the verdict on severity: only CRITICAL/HIGH findings force `REQUEST CHANGES` (in any round); MEDIUM and LOW never set the verdict — MEDIUM is orchestrator-routed (fix / send back if load-bearing / defer), so a criteria-meeting diff approves without burning the 3-round cap.
- **`scripts/gate.sh`** — run from anywhere in the tree; `cd`s to repo root, auto-detects stacks (Node/Python/Shell/Rust/Go), runs applicable checks + `gate.d/*.sh` hooks. Exit 0 = pass.
- **`scripts/export-template.sh <dest>`** — produce a clean template from HEAD with full TRIP, no instance history, and a fast consumer gate; consumer CI retains the smoke suite.
- **`scripts/demo.sh <slug> [<dir>]`** — runs the plan demonstration after the implementer gates and commits; the cold code-reviewer re-runs the branch copy and judges whether its output shows the goal. Plans require real inputs and writes only under `mktemp -d`; units with no runnable behavior may use `None: <reason>`.
- **`scripts/release.sh <slug> [--confirm-delta <text>]`** — the release driver; also `check-version` / `next-version` / `tag-after-merge` subcommands. One universal PR-merge topology: fetch-guards `origin/main` (before and after the gate), commits the bump on the release branch, never merges/tags/pushes `main`. After the PR merges, `tag-after-merge <slug>` fetches, verifies `origin/main`'s tip is this release (its `VERSION` and `Release v<version>` subject), and creates the tag locally — the tag push is `/4-release`'s final autonomous step. A release also writes the slug to `work/.last-released` (in the release commit), and `check_previous_retro` refuses the *next* release while `work/<that-slug>/retro.md` is missing or empty, or while the branch's marker is a symlink, deleted, or not byte-identical to `origin/main`'s.
- **`scripts/worktree.sh add|remove|sync-artifacts|list <slug>`** — manages worktrees under `worktrees.dir`, defaulting to `../<checkout-basename>-worktrees` (invoke add/remove from the primary checkout); `add` bases new branches on `origin/main` and records the unit's `plan.md` onto the branch; `sync-artifacts` records primary-checkout work artifacts on the unit branch without overwriting the worktree plan; **`scripts/agent-exec.sh <worktree> <handoff>`** — feeds a handoff to the implementer runtime inside one.
- **`scripts/state.sh <slug>`** — prints the work unit's derived execution state (`stage`/`review`/`next_action`) so a cold session learns where a unit stands and what comes next without the prior conversation; read-only, gate result out of scope by design.
- **`scripts/fan-exec.sh dispatch <slug> <handoff> <N>` / `adopt <slug> <sample-branch>`** — the best-of-N primitive behind `2-implement`'s Fan Mode; sequential (N× wall-clock), activated only when `implementer.fan > 1`.

## Conventions

- **Writer never reviews.** Reviews come only from fresh `.claude/agents/` subagents (read-only, separate model). Never review a diff produced in your own thread.
- **The gate and release are scripts, and authoritative.** Never overrule `gate.sh` or `release.sh`; enforcement never lives in skill prose.
- **Implementation happens in worktrees, never in this checkout.** Use `worktree.sh`.
- **Verdict sentinels are distinct by stage and vendor:** `Plan verdict:`, `Code-review verdict:`, `Codex-review verdict:`; `release.sh` releases only on exact `Code-review verdict: APPROVE` **and** `Codex-review verdict: APPROVE` lines in the branch's plan. Exit ≥2 from `codex-review.sh` is a tooling error, never a verdict — no sentinel may be recorded from artifact prose after a non-zero exit.
- **Release preconditions** (all before anything irreversible; trap rollback after): APPROVE sentinel, gate green, `ARCHI.md` fresher than the last commit touching `scripts/ skills/ profiles/ config.yaml CLAUDE.md` (via `scripts/archi-fresh.sh`; `/3-review` runs the same check earlier and heals staleness with a targeted branch edit, so release rarely trips it), version strictly increasing, and `origin/main` an ancestor of the release branch — re-checked after the gate to close the cross-release version collision.
- **`worktree.sh` only asks git** (`git worktree`, `git rev-parse`), never inspects `.git` directly — works from inside a worktree where `.git` is a file. Preserve this. **YAML parsing is naive awk** in `gate.sh`/`worktree.sh`/`agent-exec.sh`/`release.sh`; `gate.sh` is section-scoped, not depth-aware; replace with `yq` only if it bites.
- All shell scripts must pass `shellcheck` (enforced by the gate). Use `set -uo pipefail`.
- Vendor/model names live only in `config.yaml`. Reviewer models are additionally in agent frontmatter, which is authoritative; config.yaml documents intent.

## Verification

`bash scripts/gate.sh` — required-tool preflight, shellcheck over all tracked `*.sh`, plus the `gate.d/` hook running `tests/test-export.sh` and `tests/test-scripts.sh` (including the worktree-dir basename fallback, the gate tool-preflight cases, the PR-merge release flow + `tag-after-merge`, the marker-integrity matrix, the dual-sentinel refusals, the hermetic fan-exec, agent-exec, codex-review, and demo suites (section/fence scoping, cwd, plain bash, exit-code matrix and dirty-tree refusal), the `worktree.sh add` base/seed cases, the `worktree.sh sync-artifacts` artifact-capture cases, and the `nb-clean.sh` / `ds-hygiene.sh` example-hook cases). CI runs the gate on push. Currently green.
work/ export-ignore
template/ export-ignore
scripts/export-template.sh export-ignore
tests/test-export.sh export-ignore
scripts/gate.d/test-scripts.sh export-ignore
.gitattributes export-ignore
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Run the first shell block in a plan's Demonstration section.
set -uo pipefail

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
Usage: scripts/demo.sh <slug> [<dir>]

Runs the plan's Demonstration from <dir> (default .).
Exit 0 = pass, 1 = demo failure, 2 = plan or tooling error.
USAGE
}
error() { echo "demo: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
if [[ "${1:-}" == --help || "${1:-}" == -h ]]; then
  usage
  exit 0
fi
if [[ $# -lt 1 || $# -gt 2 ]]; then
  usage >&2
  exit 2
fi
dir=${2:-.}
[[ $(git -C "$dir" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree 2>/dev/null) == true ]] || error "$dir is not a git work tree"
plan="$dir/work/$1/plan.md"
[[ -f "$plan" ]] || error "no plan: $plan"
block=$(mktemp) || error "cannot create temporary file"
trap 'rm -f "$block"' EXIT
section=0
fence=
capture=0
found=0
fence_re='^(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)$'
while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n "$line" ]]; do
  if [[ -n "$fence" ]]; then
    close_re="^${fence}[${fence:0:1}]*[[:space:]]*$"
    if [[ "$line" =~ $close_re ]]; then
      fence=
      if (( capture )); then found=1; break; fi
    elif (( capture )); then
      printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$block" || error "cannot write temporary file"
    fi
    continue
  fi
  if [[ "$line" == '## Demonstration' ]]; then
    section=1
    continue
  fi
  if (( section )) && [[ "$line" == '## '* ]]; then break; fi
  if [[ "$line" =~ $fence_re ]]; then
    fence=${BASH_REMATCH[1]}
    language=${BASH_REMATCH[2]}
    if (( section )) && [[ "$fence" == '```' ]] && [[ "$language" == sh || "$language" == bash ]]; then
      capture=1
    fi
  elif (( section )) && [[ "$line" == 'None: '* ]]; then
    echo "demo: none — ${line#None: }"
    exit 0
  fi
done <"$plan"
(( section )) || error 'no ## Demonstration section'
(( found )) || error 'no shell block or None: in ## Demonstration'
before=$(git -C "$dir" status --porcelain) || error "cannot read worktree status"
(cd "$dir" && bash "$block")
status=$?
after=$(git -C "$dir" status --porcelain) || error "cannot read worktree status"
verdict=0
if (( status != 0 )); then
